🧪 Drill #1 — Wi-Fi / Access Point DHCP Failure

Difficulty: Easy–Medium

Scenario

User cannot access the internet; Wi-Fi says “Connected, no internet.”

Environment

Windows 10 laptop

Corporate Wi-Fi

Other users unaffected

Troubleshooting Steps

Ran ipconfig /all → APIPA 169.254 address.

Tried ipconfig /renew → DHCP unreachable.

Wired test works → DHCP server healthy.

Tested another Wi-Fi client → also fails.

Inspected AP’s switch port → uplink cable loose.

Reseated cable → AP regains DHCP connectivity.

Root Cause

Loose AP uplink cable preventing DHCP traffic.

Lesson Learned

If Wi-Fi has APIPA and wired works → check AP’s uplink first.

🧪 Drill #2 — Shared Drive Not Accessible (DNS Issue)

Difficulty: Medium

Scenario

User cannot access mapped drive S: → \\FILE-SRV01\Shared.

Troubleshooting Steps

ping FILE-SRV01 fails;

ping <server IP> works;

ipconfig /all shows DNS = 8.8.8.8 (public).

Reset DNS to internal servers.

Ping hostname works; drive reconnects.

Root Cause

Client using public DNS, unable to resolve internal hostnames.

Lesson Learned

If IP works but hostname doesn’t → it’s DNS. Always DNS.

🧪 Drill #3 — Outlook Stuck on “Loading Profile”

Difficulty: Medium–Hard

Scenario

Outlook hangs indefinitely on startup.

Troubleshooting Steps

OWA works → mailbox/server OK.

Killed hung Outlook processes.

Profile repair fails → deeper issue.

Safe Mode fails → not add-ins.

Autodiscover reset fails.

Renamed OST file → Outlook rebuilds → launches successfully.

Root Cause

Corrupted OST file.

Lesson Learned

OWA vs desktop Outlook instantly isolates profile/cache corruption.

🧪 Drill #4 — “Network Slow” (Actually: Failing HDD)

Difficulty: Medium–Spicy

Scenario

User claims the network is slow, but entire system is lagging.

Troubleshooting Steps

Task Manager → Disk 100%.

Defender scanning aggressively.

Event Viewer → disk I/O errors + bad blocks.

Checked Windows Security → no malware.

Backed up user data.

Scheduled drive replacement.

Root Cause

Failing HDD causing system-wide stalls and disk timeouts.

Lesson Learned

Not every “network problem” is the network — check system health.

🧪 Drill #5 — “Password Incorrect” (Local Login Trick)

Difficulty: Spicy–Hard

Scenario

User insists their password is correct but cannot log in.

Troubleshooting Steps

Confirmed other users can log in → AD healthy.

Login screen shows Sign in to: This device (local login).

Checked Ethernet cable → loose; no domain connectivity.

Reseated cable → domain reachable.

User logs in successfully.

Root Cause

PC defaulted to local login due to no network/domain connection.

Lesson Learned

“Password incorrect” often = wrong login scope, not wrong password.

🧪 Drill #6 — VPN Connects but No Internet or Internal Access

Difficulty: Hard

Scenario

User connects VPN successfully, but loses ALL connectivity.

Troubleshooting Steps

Baseline internet works without VPN.

Compared ipconfig before/after VPN.

After VPN:

DNS = 0.0.0.0

Gateway missing

Wi-Fi shows “Media disconnected”

ipconfig /flushdns / registerdns → no change.

Reset VPN virtual adapter:

netsh int ip reset
netsh winsock reset


Reinstalled VPN client.

VPN reconnect → proper DNS and routes restored.

Root Cause

Corrupted VPN virtual adapter pushing invalid DNS and routing.

Lesson Learned

VPN issues are usually routing/DNS, not the VPN server.

🧪 Drill #7 — Intermittent Wi-Fi Drops Every 5–10 Minutes

Difficulty: Very Spicy

Scenario

Wi-Fi stays “connected” but traffic drops frequently for 20–30 seconds.

Troubleshooting Steps

User tests near different AP → no drops.

Continuous ping shows intermittent traffic loss.

User reports issue worse when plugged in → power clue.

Checked Wi-Fi adapter power settings →

“Allow computer to turn off this device” = enabled.

Checked Power Plan → Wireless set to “Maximum Power Saving.”

Set both battery + AC to Maximum Performance.

Traffic stable; drops resolved.

Root Cause

Power-saving mode throttling Wi-Fi adapter, cutting traffic intermittently.

Lesson Learned

Power-saving features can cripple Wi-Fi even when signal looks strong
